# AWOW AK41 always-on core — configuration.
#
# Copy this to `.env` (same directory) and fill in the values. The autoinstall
# first-boot flow writes a `.env` from these placeholders (see autoinstall/).
# NOTHING here is committed — .env is gitignored; only THIS .example is tracked.
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER — fill with your own; never commit a real one.
#
# QUOTING RULE: this file is read BOTH by docker compose AND shell-sourced by
# firstboot.sh / provision-technitium.sh — any value containing a SPACE must be
# double-quoted (`KEY="a b"`), or first boot dies at the source line (found
# 2026-07-10; compose strips the quotes, so quoting is safe for both readers).

# ── Domains & network ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public apex domain your router forwards to this box.
DOMAIN=example.tld
# LAN IP of THIS AWOW box. Split-horizon records point the subdomains here so
# home devices connect directly and TLS still validates.
# Give the box a DHCP reservation / static lease at this address.
LAN_IP=192.168.1.10
# LAN IP of the flaky MAIN box (Minecraft/BlueMap/backups). Used by the
# commented reverse-proxy blocks in the Caddyfile. Blank if none.
MAIN_BOX_IP=192.168.1.20
# Hostname Technitium advertises for itself.
DNS_HOSTNAME=dns.example.tld

# ── Subdomains (each gets a split-horizon A record → LAN_IP) ───────────────
TRACKER_SUBDOMAIN=tracker
ACTUAL_SUBDOMAIN=actual
# 'dns' (Technitium console) and, optionally, a BlueMap subdomain proxied to the
# main box are handled in the Caddyfile / provisioning list.

# ── Caddy / TLS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Email for Let's Encrypt / ZeroSSL account (ACME). Required for public certs.
ACME_EMAIL=you@example.tld
# basic_auth for the SINGLE-USER services (Actual, Technitium console). The
# multi-user tracker is NOT basic_auth'd — oauth2-proxy owns its auth (below).
# Each value is a Caddy-compatible bcrypt hash. Reference generator:
#   docker run --rm caddy:2-alpine caddy hash-password --plaintext 'yourpass'
# (Deployments driven by the Personal secret pipeline mint the plaintext AND
# derive this hash automatically — PrepDeploySecrets.ps1, 2026-07-30; the
# plaintext for the browser prompt is read back with Show-DeploySecret.ps1.)
#
# !! DOUBLE EVERY `$` IN THESE HASHES !!  `$2a$14$abc…` must be written
# `$$2a$$14$$abc…`. Docker Compose interpolates `$VAR` inside .env VALUES, so
# an un-doubled bcrypt hash whose salt begins with a letter is read as a
# variable reference and replaced with NOTHING: Caddy receives `$2a$14` and
# rejects every login, with only a "variable is not set" warning to show for
# it. Salts are base64, so ~81% of generated hashes hit this — and which ones
# changes every time you regenerate. Compose collapses `$$` back to one `$`,
# verified end-to-end. Materialize-Deploy.ps1 does this for you; only hand-
# edits need care. (Single-quoting also works, but breaks outright on a value
# containing a single quote, so `$$` is the rule everywhere.)
ACTUAL_BASICAUTH_USER=admin
ACTUAL_BASICAUTH_HASH=REPLACE_WITH_caddy_hash-password_OUTPUT
DNS_BASICAUTH_USER=admin
DNS_BASICAUTH_HASH=REPLACE_WITH_caddy_hash-password_OUTPUT

# WHO MAY REACH THE GUARDED SINGLE-USER SITES AT ALL (added 2026-08-29).
# actual.<domain> and dns.<domain> are wrapped in a `remote_ip` matcher; anything
# outside this gets 403 BEFORE basic_auth, so a WAN caller is never even told
# there is a prompt. The router forwards :80/:443, so without this those two
# hosts answer an unlimited-guess prompt to the whole internet.
#
# `private_ranges` is Caddy's built-in shorthand for every RFC1918 block, which
# is the honest expression of "LAN or VPN, not the internet" and covers a future
# WireGuard subnet with no further edit. Narrow it to a single CIDR only if you
# want to distinguish devices WITHIN the LAN - that is a different question, and
# basic_auth is what answers it.
#
# It does NOT affect certificates: issuance is dns-01 through Cloudflare, so
# Let's Encrypt never needs an inbound path (see stack/caddy/Dockerfile).
LAN_CIDR=private_ranges

# ── oauth2-proxy (Google sign-in for the tracker subdomain) — WI-10.2 / D1/D2 ─
#
# +--------------------- OWNER MANUAL STEP (required) ---------------------+
# | Create a Google Cloud OAuth 2.0 Client BEFORE first bring-up:          |
# |  1. console.cloud.google.com -> APIs & Services -> Credentials.        |
# |  2. Configure the OAuth consent screen (External; add each allowed     |
# |     Google account as a Test user while unverified, OR publish it).    |
# |     Scopes: openid, email, profile (the slacker-tracker pattern the    |
# |     Owner trusts uses `email profile`).                                |
# |  3. Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID -> type "Web application".   |
# |  4. Under "Authorized redirect URIs" register EXACTLY:                 |
# |        https://tracker.<your-domain>/oauth2/callback                    |
# |     (substitute TRACKER_SUBDOMAIN + DOMAIN; e.g.                        |
# |        https://tracker.example.tld/oauth2/callback ).                   |
# |     "Authorized JavaScript origins" is not required (server-side flow). |
# |  5. Paste the generated Client ID + Client secret below.              |
# +------------------------------------------------------------------------+
# PIN BUMPED 2026-07-10 (review): v7.6.0 → v7.15.2. Multiple auth-bypass
# advisories were fixed between those tags (CVE-2026-34457, CVE-2026-40575,
# GHSA-7x63-xv5r-3p2x, GHSA-pxq7-h93f-9jrg, email-validation GHSA-c5c4-8r6x-56w3)
# and this is the internet-facing front door. NOT yet sim-validated at this tag:
# re-run the homehub-sim (V1) + vmtest/export-images.sh before flashing real
# hardware (pin ledger: docs/status.md).
OAUTH2_PROXY_IMAGE_TAG=v7.15.2
OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_WITH_GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID.apps.googleusercontent.com
OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET
# 32-byte cookie encryption seed. Generate:
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -- '+/' '-_'
OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_openssl_rand_base64_32
# Allow-list of Google accounts permitted through (Q10.5: explicit list to start;
# a remote-settable console is a deferred FUTURE knob, not built). Comma- or
# space-separated. The first-boot flow writes each entry, one per line, into
# oauth2-proxy/authenticated-emails.txt (gitignored) which the container reads.
OAUTH2_PROXY_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@gmail.com,other-allowed@gmail.com

# ── Office-wall kiosk site (SR-016 / OI-12, RATIFIED 2026-07-29) ───────────
# The wall panel is a keyboard-less ambient display: it cannot complete a Google
# consent, so it gets its OWN Caddy site that injects its identity by source IP
# instead of asking it to sign in. Read the Caddyfile banner before changing any
# of these four — they are the whole security model.
#
# +----------------------- DO NOT FORWARD THIS PORT -----------------------+
# | WALL_PORT is served on the LAN ONLY. The router forwards :80/:443 and  |
# | NOTHING ELSE. The hostname below resolves PUBLICLY (the DDNS wildcard  |
# | covers *.DOMAIN), so the only thing keeping the panel's no-login       |
# | identity off the internet is (a) no forward for this port, (b) the     |
# | LAN_IP-bound publish in docker-compose.yml, and (c) the /32 match.     |
# +------------------------------------------------------------------------+
#
# Hostname the panel loads. A subdomain of DOMAIN so the wildcard DDNS record
# and the ACME cert both already cover it. Add its bare label to
# EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS (below) so provisioning creates the split-horizon A record.
WALL_HOST=wall.example.tld
# LAN-only HTTPS port for that site. Any high port; keep it off :443 so a
# mis-configured router forward cannot reach it. (T0 — the default is fine.)
WALL_PORT=8443
# The panel's IP, DHCP-RESERVED on its hardware MAC. The site's allow-list is
# this address and nothing else (/32 — not the LAN CIDR: guest Wi-Fi, IoT gear
# and an inbound-facing Minecraft server share that network). If the panel's
# address moves, the site 403s it — that is the intended failure direction.
PANEL_IP=192.168.1.50
# NagLight identity the kiosk site injects as X-Forwarded-User after STRIPPING
# any client-supplied one. This is the Owner's own stable Google `sub` (D-W7:
# the panel gets the full interactive view), so panel check-offs are his.
PANEL_USER_SUB=REPLACE_WITH_GOOGLE_SUB_OF_THE_PANEL_USER

# ── Technitium DNS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PINNED (Q10.9 B+): concrete version baked into the ISO payload and docker-loaded
# at first boot. This is the exact image the homehub-sim validated (V1: latest ==
# 15.2.0, amd64 manifest verified byte-identical). Bump deliberately + re-export.
TECHNITIUM_IMAGE_TAG=15.2.0
# Initial admin password (set on first boot; provision script is idempotent).
TECHNITIUM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD
# API token NAME the provisioning script creates/reuses for headless health/config.
# The token VALUE is generated by Technitium and written to provision/.token
# (gitignored) for later curl-based health checks.
TECHNITIUM_API_TOKEN_NAME=automation
# Upstream forwarders for recursion fallback / DoH. Quad9 DoH by default;
# space-separated (KEEP the double quotes when adding more — QUOTING RULE).
TECHNITIUM_FORWARDERS="https://dns.quad9.net/dns-query"
# Blocklist URLs (space-separated, quoted). Same lists Pi-hole/AdGuard consume.
TECHNITIUM_BLOCKLISTS="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/StevenBlack/hosts/master/hosts"

# ── tracker service (NagLight web container) ───────────────────────────────
# The image is built LOCALLY from a sibling NagLight checkout (Q10.2, no registry
# yet):  docker build -t naglight:local ../NagLight
# Git remote of the DATA repo (your private definitions+logs repo).
# The tracker container clones this into its /data volume on first boot and
# `git pull --rebase` on later boots. Leave blank to manage /data manually.
# NOTE: in multi-user mode (TRACKER_MULTI_USER=true) leave this BLANK and keep
# TRACKER_COMMIT=false — hosted users get per-user data dirs, git stays off (D3).
TRACKER_DATA_REMOTE=
# Identity the tracker uses for its check-off / nightly-materialize commits.
# (Quoted: contains a space — see the QUOTING RULE in the header.)
TRACKER_GIT_NAME="naglight bot"
TRACKER_GIT_EMAIL=tracker@example.tld
# When true, `tracker serve --commit` — the nightly materialize + check-offs
# commit (and, if the remote is set, push) to the data repo. Single-user only.
TRACKER_COMMIT=true
# Trusted-header multi-user mode (D3, NagLight WI-10.5). true = identity comes
# ONLY from oauth2-proxy's X-Forwarded-Email header; per-user data dir by Google
# `sub`. Keep TRACKER_COMMIT=false and TRACKER_DATA_REMOTE blank when true.
TRACKER_MULTI_USER=false
# Multi-user only: the directory whose `definitions/` seed a BRAND-NEW user the
# first time one appears. Blank = off, which is the right production default —
# a real household's definitions arrive with the person, not from a fixture.
#
# The A19 two-VM gate is why this is a knob at all. Measured 2026-08-01: a gate
# hub ran `healthy` for hours with an EMPTY /data, because the healthcheck
# probes /healthz, which is deliberately data-free (SR-040). The panel then
# renders a tracker with no items in it, and "no red drive check" looks exactly
# like "the drive check is green". The sim seed at ../sim/tracker-seed carries
# `library-mounted` and `backup-drive-mounted` — the two lanes A19 asserts on.
#
# TWO knobs because they live on opposite sides of the bind mount: _SRC is a
# host path relative to stack/, _DIR is where it lands in the container. The
# default _SRC points at a directory that ships with no definitions/, so a box
# that sets neither seeds nothing and a box that sets only _SRC still seeds
# nothing.
TRACKER_SEED_DIR=
TRACKER_SEED_SRC=./tracker-seed
# ── One-way traceability mirror (NagLight SN-024, the Owner 2026-08-09) ──────
# A record of how ONE person's tracked items changed over time, pushed to a
# PRIVATE git repo. The ruling it is built to: the upstream service remains the
# source of truth, and git is traceability only — never a second authority and
# never a restore path. So the mirror only ever pushes; it fetches the remote's
# HISTORY once (so commits continue the chain) and never its CONTENT, and if the
# remote gains commits this box lacks it reports and stops rather than
# force-pushing or pulling.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE SETTING IT ALONGSIDE TRACKER_DATA_REMOTE ABOVE: they point in
# OPPOSITE directions. That one pulls the remote INTO /data (single-user only);
# this one pushes /data OUT. Setting both is incoherent — pick one.
#
# Blank = off, and off is the shipped default.
TRACKER_MIRROR_REMOTE=
# REQUIRED whenever the remote is set and TRACKER_MULTI_USER=true: the ONE
# identity mirrored, as its stable Google `sub` — the same value as
# NAGLIGHT_USER / PANEL_USER_SUB. With the remote set and this blank the mirror
# REFUSES TO START and logs why, deliberately: defaulting to "the data dir"
# would push every household member's private data into one repo.
TRACKER_MIRROR_USER=
# Branch (default main) and cycle period (default 1h). Hourly is the Owner's
# call — a commit per change was judged too noisy to read back.
TRACKER_MIRROR_BRANCH=
TRACKER_MIRROR_INTERVAL=
# Repo token. A GitHub fine-grained PAT scoped to `contents: write` on THAT ONE
# private repo is enough — no other scope is used, so a leak cannot reach
# anything else. Never written to .git/config, the remote URL, argv or a log.
TRACKER_MIRROR_TOKEN=

# ── Drive/Sheet config sync (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────
# OFF unless the client id/secret, token dir, public URL AND a source are all
# set. The client id/secret are NOT knobs here: compose reuses the
# OAUTH2_PROXY_* pair so there is one credential to rotate, not two.
#
# The ONE identity the sync runs for in multi-user mode. Blank = sync OFF and
# connect/callback only; the tracker will not guess which household member's
# sheet to apply. Same value shape as TRACKER_MIRROR_USER (a Google `sub`).
TRACKER_DRIVE_USER=
# The spreadsheet `tracker seed-sheet` created. Blank until seeded.
TRACKER_DRIVE_SHEET_ID=
# A folder source INSTEAD of a sheet; normally blank. `root` is accepted only to
# bootstrap the first connect and is REFUSED as a sync source.
TRACKER_DRIVE_FOLDER_ID=

# ── Finance-Auditor — daily finance audit pipeline (SR-014, IF-004) ─────────
# OPT-IN until Finance-Auditor passes its G-Release/G-Final: enable by adding
# `finance-auditor` to COMPOSE_PROFILES (below). Image is built from the
# private sibling repo by scripts/ensure-local-images.sh, with the
# @actual-app/api version pinned to ACTUAL_IMAGE_TAG (IF-002 coupling —
# re-run the resolver with --rebuild whenever the Actual pin bumps).
FINANCE_AUDITOR_IMAGE_TAG=local
# Public fallback once FA publishes an image (same chain as TRACKER_PUBLIC_IMAGE).
FINANCE_AUDITOR_PUBLIC_IMAGE=
# Daily run time, BOX-LOCAL (TIMEZONE below governs how this is read).
FINANCE_RUN_AT=05:00
# Actual budget Sync ID (Actual: Settings -> Advanced -> Sync ID). Not a
# secret. OPTIONAL: empty = the auditor auto-discovers it at startup (sole
# budget file on the server; several = fatal naming them). Set only to pin
# one budget on a multi-budget server.
FINANCE_ACTUAL_SYNC_ID=
# Actual's SERVER password. Direction reversed 2026-07-30 (A9d): minted on the
# dev PC and SET on the un-bootstrapped server by provision-actual.sh at first
# boot (no UI step); browser logins read it back via Show-DeploySecret.ps1.
# Hand-managed installs may still set it in Actual's UI and paste it here.
FINANCE_ACTUAL_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_ACTUAL_SERVER_PASSWORD
# Bearer token for the tracker's /api/feed (TRACKER_API_TOKEN on the tracker).
FINANCE_TRACKER_FEED_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_TRACKER_API_TOKEN

# ── Auxiliary LAN-only observability (WI-10.11) ────────────────────────────
# Each UI is published bound to LAN_IP only (reachable on the LAN, never proxied
# to the public internet). Change the host ports if they collide with something.
# Uptime-Kuma (generic up-checks; can POST to NagLight /api/feed).
# PINNED (Q10.9 B+): concrete tag baked into the payload (was the floating `1`).
UPTIMEKUMA_IMAGE_TAG=1.23.17
UPTIMEKUMA_PORT=3001
# Dozzle (live container log viewer; reads the docker socket read-only).
# PINNED (Q10.9 B+): concrete tag baked into the payload (was the floating `v8`).
DOZZLE_IMAGE_TAG=v8.14.12
DOZZLE_PORT=8081
# ntfy (OPTIONAL self-hosted push; opt-in via `docker compose --profile ntfy up`).
# PINNED (Q10.9 B+): concrete tag baked into the payload (was floating `latest`).
NTFY_IMAGE_TAG=v2.25.0
NTFY_PORT=8090

# ── Dynamic DNS (Cloudflare) — AWOW owns DDNS per WI-9 Q4 ──────────────────
# Keeps the apex + wildcard (*.DOMAIN) A records pointed at the home
# connection, checked every DDNS_PERIOD. Replaces the legacy
# DDNS-Cloudflare-PowerShell scripts that ran on Mini-serv.
# Create a NEW scoped API token (permissions: Zone -> DNS -> Edit, only for
# this zone) at dash.cloudflare.com -> My Profile -> API Tokens.
# Do NOT reuse the old token — the one that sat in PLAINTEXT on the legacy
# Windows box's setup share is flagged for rotation (WI-9.1 secrets finding).
# PINNED (Q10.9 B+): concrete release baked into the payload (was floating `latest`).
DDNS_IMAGE_TAG=v2.10.0
# Check/update cadence ("a few minutes").
DDNS_PERIOD=5m
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=REPLACE_WITH_CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_NEW_SCOPED_DNS_EDIT_TOKEN

# ── Image tags (PINNED for reproducibility — Q10.9 B+ ALL-IMAGES) ───────────
# Every tag here is a CONCRETE version that is docker-saved into the ISO deploy
# payload and docker-loaded at first boot (vmtest/export-images.sh + firstboot.sh),
# so what boots == what the homehub-sim validated. Floating tags (`latest`, `2-alpine`,
# `v8`) are now WRONG here: they let first boot drift from the validated set. Bump
# a pin ONLY deliberately, then re-run export-images.sh and rebuild the payload.
# The full pin set + registry digests are recorded in docs/status.md.
# TAGS caddy-cloudflare, NOT caddy. Since 2026-08-08 the stack runs a Caddy built
# from stack/caddy/Dockerfile with the caddy-dns/cloudflare provider compiled in,
# because ACME's dns-01 challenge is the only one that works here: http-01 needs
# Let's Encrypt to reach this box on :80 from the public internet, and with no
# router forward NOTHING was ever issued — Caddy then served no certificate at
# all and every HTTPS site returned nothing. scripts/ensure-local-images.sh
# builds it and derives the -builder stage's version by stripping this tag's
# suffix, so keep the `<version>-<variant>` shape.
CADDY_IMAGE_TAG=2.11.4-alpine
# Local NagLight image tag (built with `docker build -t naglight:local ../NagLight`).
# Stays `local`: no registry home (Q10.2) — this is THE image the AWOW can never
# pull, so export-images.sh fails loudly if it isn't built before baking the payload.
TRACKER_IMAGE_TAG=local
# PUBLIC fallback for the tracker image (SR-006 resolver chain): NagLight is a
# PRIVATE sibling repo, so scripts/ensure-local-images.sh resolves naglight:local
# as present -> sibling build (../NagLight) -> THIS public image (pull + retag).
# Empty = no public image published yet; set (e.g. ghcr.io/<owner>/naglight:<tag>)
# once the app repo publishes, and anyone can run the sim with no sibling checkout.
TRACKER_PUBLIC_IMAGE=
ACTUAL_IMAGE_TAG=26.7.0

# ── FileBackup — the LIBRARY backup container (E2 / P1.3) ───────────────────
# CONSUMED BY THE `filebackup` ONE-SHOT AND NOTHING ELSE. That service sits
# behind the `filebackup` profile, and the profile is deliberately NOT in
# COMPOSE_PROFILES below and must never be added to it: firstboot runs a plain
# `docker compose up -d`, and a profiled-in backup service would start a
# multi-day library backup on a freshly imaged box. The only caller is
# stack/backup/library-backup.sh, on the homehub-library-backup.timer:
#     docker compose --profile filebackup run --rm -T filebackup backup
# The image still reaches the ISO payload — vmtest/export-images.sh bakes this
# profile unconditionally, the same way it bakes ntfy.
#
# Local build, no registry home (the naglight:local pattern): built from a
# sibling ../FileBackup checkout by scripts/ensure-local-images.sh, which
# stamps homehub.source.revision so export-images.sh can refuse a stale bake.
# Build it with --rebuild — the resolver SKIPS an image that already exists,
# and a hand-tagged one would short-circuit the build and leave no stamp.
FILEBACKUP_IMAGE_TAG=local
# The five HOST paths the container binds. Each is also defaulted inline in
# docker-compose.yml (an empty value would make `:/source:ro`, which breaks the
# whole compose file, not just this service) — these are the declarations, the
# compose defaults are the floor. Every path INSIDE the container is fixed by
# stack/backup/filebackup.json and derives from nothing site-specific.
#
# The library, mounted READ-ONLY into the container. Its fstab umask=0002
# already grants world-read, so the non-root container can read it as shipped.
FILEBACKUP_SOURCE=/srv/library
# Manifest/hash cache — SYSTEM disk, deliberately: small, and it must outlive
# the backup drive being unplugged.
FILEBACKUP_STATE=/var/lib/homehub-filebackup/state
# The browsable mirror (Q-FB1) and the Snapshot_<date> history, both on the
# backup drive. Q-FB5: that drive mounts uid=65532,gid=65532 so the non-root
# container can write it; the wrapper write-probes both before every run.
FILEBACKUP_BACKUP=/mnt/backup-drive/library
FILEBACKUP_CHANGES=/mnt/backup-drive/library-changes
# The container's own log directory (Backup_Global.log). System disk.
FILEBACKUP_LOGS=/var/log/homehub-filebackup
# The 7-Zip EFFORT level for files FileBackup decides to compress - 7-Zip's own
# -mx range, a single digit 0-9 (FileBackup kit revision 12, 2026-09-01). It
# never changes WHETHER a file is compressed, only how hard 7-Zip tries, and
# every level's archive restores with the same kit.
#
# 5 IS THE RULING, NOT THE IMAGE'S DEFAULT (the Owner, 2026-09-01): FileBackup
# itself falls back to 9 when this is unset, which is 7-Zip "Ultra" - the level
# that was re-packing a 4 TB library at maximum effort for a few percent. 5 is
# 7-Zip "Normal": most of the ratio for a fraction of the CPU and wall clock.
# Passed to the container as an environment variable by docker-compose.yml,
# which also defaults it to 5 inline, so a materialised .env that predates this
# knob still gets Normal rather than silently falling back to Ultra.
FILEBACKUP_7Z_LEVEL=5

# ── Host / timezone (used by autoinstall) ──────────────────────────────────
TIMEZONE=Etc/UTC

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TIER-2 OPT-IN CATALOG (SR-012) — everything below configures services that are
# OFF by default (compose profiles; see the docker-compose.yml tier-2 banner and
# stack/README §9). Filling these in changes NOTHING until a profile is enabled.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Which opt-in profiles start (comma-separated). This is THE enable switch:
# compose reads it from .env, so firstboot's plain `docker compose up -d` brings
# these up too — set it BEFORE building the USB and the box boots with them.
# Empty = core stack only. Photo stacks: run at most ONE of immich/photoprism;
# immich-ml is separate and heavy — leave it off on the J4125, or run it with
# IMMICH_ML_MEM_LIMIT set (below) so it can only ever OOM itself.
#   e.g. COMPOSE_PROFILES=navidrome,audiobookshelf,vaultwarden,diun,ntfy
#
# `gunmaster3` MUST NOT APPEAR HERE, AND PUTTING IT HERE IS A DEFECT rather than
# an alternative way of switching the relay on. The crossplay relay's enable
# knob is GAME_RELAY_ENABLED below; its compose `profiles:` entry exists solely
# to keep it OUT of firstboot's bulk `docker compose up -d`, and only
# homehub-gunmaster3-relay.service ever passes `--profile gunmaster3`. Naming it
# here would let the bulk `up` start a public, unauthenticated service outside
# the unit that fences it. Refused (not warned about) by verify-hub.sh
# (TC-H-T07), scripts/ensure-local-images.sh and vmtest/export-images.sh.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# HOST directory media libraries live under (bind-mounted into the media
# services; subdirs: music/ podcasts/ audiobooks/ photos/ immich/). Docker
# creates missing dirs, but the POINT is to aim this at real storage — NOT the
# WI-10.10 backup drives (serving media would defeat their spin-down policy).
#
# CASE MATTERS when this points at a real library: the subdir names above are
# lowercase, and both ntfs3 and ext4 are case-SENSITIVE, so a `Music` folder is
# not found by a `music` bind mount — docker silently creates an empty
# lowercase sibling instead. Match the on-disk casing exactly.
#
# IF THIS PATH IS ON A MOUNTED DRIVE, it must be mounted BEFORE compose starts,
# or docker creates the bind sources on the system disk and the real mount then
# shadows them (firstboot step 3b handles this; see the note there).
MEDIA_ROOT=/srv/media

# Extra split-horizon subdomains (space/comma-separated bare labels), each gets
# an A record → LAN_IP at provisioning. Add a label when you uncomment its
# Caddyfile site — QUOTE it (QUOTING RULE): EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS="vault photos music"
# ALSO put WALL_HOST's bare label here (e.g. "wall"): the kiosk site is always
# on, so the panel needs the split-horizon record to reach it over the LAN
# rather than hairpinning through the router.
# AND GAME_HOST's bare label (e.g. "game") when GAME_RELAY_ENABLED is true —
# see the block below for why that record is worth more than convenience.
EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS=

# ── Gun Master 3 crossplay relay (GAME_RELAY_ENABLED) ────────────────────────
# The family relay so the Owner and his son can see each other in Gun Master 3
# from different houses. Source: FinnsGame/server. OFF unless
# GAME_RELAY_ENABLED=true below.
#
# ── IT IS NOT A COMPOSE PROFILE, AND THAT IS THE 2026-08-29 REFRESH ──────────
# Every other opt-in service on this box is switched on by naming its profile in
# COMPOSE_PROFILES. This one is not, because DOCKER DOES NOT OWN ITS LIFECYCLE:
# `homehub-gunmaster3-relay.service` does, and that unit `Requires=` the host
# fence (`homehub-game-isolation.service`) so that systemd — not shell branching
# in firstboot — both orders the fence first and propagates its failure. The
# compose service keeps `restart: "no"` and `profiles: ["gunmaster3"]`, which
# together mean nothing but that unit can ever start the container.
# Full reasoning: HomeHub's CROSSPLAY_HANDOFF.md §7.
#
# +--------------------- THIS ONE IS DELIBERATELY PUBLIC ------------------+
# | Every other password-guarded surface on this box was taken OFF the     |
# | internet on 2026-08-29. This one is the exception, by ruling: the      |
# | whole requirement is playing together while away from home and NOT on  |
# | the VPN. Its door is a SECRET PATH, not an identity check — the        |
# | Caddyfile's game block carries the full reasoning and the honest       |
# | account of what that is worth. Read it before changing any of these.   |
# +------------------------------------------------------------------------+
#
# THE ENABLE SWITCH. `true` makes homehub-gunmaster3-relay.service start the
# relay (behind its fence); anything else — including unset — makes that unit's
# ExecCondition report "condition not met", which systemd records as a CLEAN
# SKIP rather than a failure, so an unused relay never shows red anywhere.
# Same convention as REMOTE_UI_ENABLED and IMMICH_ML_ENABLED. (T0.)
#
# Turning it on afterwards, on a running box, is three commands and no reflash:
#   sed -i 's/^GAME_RELAY_ENABLED=.*/GAME_RELAY_ENABLED=true/' .env
#   systemctl start homehub-gunmaster3-relay
#   bash scripts/verify/verify-hub.sh   # TC-H-T06 + TC-H-T07 from HomeHub
GAME_RELAY_ENABLED=false
#
# Hostname the game connects to. A subdomain of DOMAIN so the wildcard DDNS
# record and the ACME cert already cover it.
GAME_HOST=game.example.tld
#
# THE DOOR. 43 base64url characters (32 random bytes), T3-Generated: minted by
# PrepDeploySecrets.ps1 via New-RandomToken, which already emits base64url
# (+ -> -, / -> _, = stripped) so the value drops into a URL path unescaped.
# Never prompted, never displayed.
#
# THIS LINE EXISTS SO THE EMITTER HAS A TEMPLATE KEY TO FILL — a knob with no
# template line is emitted by nothing and reported by nothing (the
# OPERATOR_PASSWORD lesson, open-items E1).
#
# TREAT THE RESULTING URL AS A PASSWORD. It is a bearer token: it identifies
# nobody and works from any address. If it leaks, rotate this value,
# re-materialise, redeploy, and re-paste on each device — that is a complete
# revocation and it costs one evening's inconvenience.
GAME_WS_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_GAME_WS_SECRET
#
# Players allowed at once; extras are told `full` and disconnected. Small on
# purpose — with no identity check, the room size IS the blast radius of a
# leaked URL. Raise it only when there is a reason. (T0.)
GAME_MAX_PLAYERS=4
#
# THE RELAY'S OWN DOOR — ruled 2026-08-30 (the Owner, open-items A29). Optional,
# and it SHIPS BLANK. Blank is safe rather than merely tolerated: the relay reads
# it as `?.trim() ?? ""` and treats empty as "no key configured -> open relay",
# which is the posture this box has had since the relay landed. It says so on
# startup, verbatim: "no connect key — open to anyone who reaches it".
#
# TWO DOORS, NOT ONE. GAME_WS_SECRET above guards the URL; this guards the
# BUILD. Set it and a leaked URL alone stops being enough to connect.
#
# ROTATION IS THE EXPENSIVE PART, which is why the game carries two keys and the
# relay only knows this one:
#   this key      -> a redeploy AND a game rebuild, in that order
#   the ROOM key  -> a game rebuild and nothing else; the relay never sees it
# The Owner's stated intent is that this one does NOT rotate. Note the trap: a
# build still carrying the old value cannot connect after a rotation, and the
# person holding that build may be a child on another machine.
#
# THREE NAMES, ONE VALUE, and they are deliberately not unified: this knob,
# GM3_CONNECT_KEY in the game build's .env, and `key` on the hello frame. The
# relay REFUSES TO START if it finds GAME_ROOM_KEY (a retired merged name) or a
# prefix-stripped CONNECT_KEY, because either would come up open while looking
# configured. (T3.)
GAME_CONNECT_KEY=
# Label in the relay's /health body and startup log. Cosmetic. (T0.)
GAME_RELAY_NAME=homehub-gunmaster3-relay
# Build tag, and it is PINNED TO A COMMIT rather than `local` (2026-08-30, the
# Owner: "tie to the sha for the currently available relay"). `local` is not a
# version: it cannot say what is running, it cannot roll back, and a rebuild
# destroys the thing you would roll back to. Every THIRD-PARTY image in this file
# carries an exact version; this makes one of the four self-built ones match.
#
# THE SHA IS THE LAST COMMIT THAT TOUCHED THE BUILD CONTEXT (`FinnsGame/server`),
# not the repo HEAD. Deliberate: the relay's bytes depend on `server/` alone, so
# pinning to repo HEAD would churn this tag every time the GAME changes and force
# rebuilds that produce identical images. ensure-local-images.sh already reasons
# this way for its `+dirty` check, which it scopes to the build context for the
# same reason.
#
# TO MOVE IT: re-pin to the new sha and rebuild, in that order —
#   git -C ../FinnsGame log -1 --format=%h -- server/     # the new value
#   ENV_FILE=<the real .env> bash scripts/ensure-local-images.sh --rebuild
# The image is also stamped `homehub.source.revision` with the repo HEAD it was
# built from, which is a different (and coarser) fact than this tag.
GAME_RELAY_IMAGE_TAG=2b760fe
#
# THE URL THE FAMILY PASTES into the game's Multiplayer box, once per device:
#     wss://<GAME_HOST>/ws-<GAME_WS_SECRET>
# With GAME_HOST's label in EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS, split-horizon makes that ONE URL
# work in both directions: at home it resolves to LAN_IP and never leaves the
# house; away it resolves to the public IP. Same string, no second profile.

# ── tier-2 image pins ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Best-effort pins as of 2026-07 authoring — NOT sim-validated (unlike the core
# pins above) and NOT baked into the ISO payload unless you export with their
# profile (vmtest/export-images.sh EXTRA_PROFILES). VERIFY the current release
# at enable time; the diun profile watches them for updates afterwards.

# Photos option A: Immich (server + ML share one version; DB image tag comes
# from Immich's release notes — bump BOTH deliberately, they move together).
IMMICH_IMAGE_TAG=v3.0.2
IMMICH_DB_IMAGE_TAG=14-vectorchord0.4.3-pgvectors0.2.0
IMMICH_REDIS_IMAGE_TAG=9
IMMICH_PORT=2283
IMMICH_DB_USERNAME=immich
IMMICH_DB_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD
IMMICH_DB_DATABASE=immich
# true ONLY when the immich-ml profile is also enabled (semantic search + faces).
IMMICH_ML_ENABLED=false
# Hard memory ceiling for the ML container (docker `mem_limit` syntax, e.g. 2g).
# On an 8 GB box this is what keeps a model load or an initial library scan from
# OOM-killing something in the CORE stack — the kernel kills this container
# instead, and compose restarts it. Empty/0 = no limit (fine on a big box).
IMMICH_ML_MEM_LIMIT=

# Photos option B: PhotoPrism (upstream publishes `latest` as THE stable tag —
# the exception to concrete pins here; diun flags when it moves).
PHOTOPRISM_IMAGE_TAG=latest
PHOTOPRISM_DB_IMAGE_TAG=11
PHOTOPRISM_PORT=2342
PHOTOPRISM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD
PHOTOPRISM_DB_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD

# Video: Jellyfin. QSV transcode needs /dev/dri, which firstboot detects and
# wires up via a generated docker-compose.override.yml — nothing to set here.
# JELLYFIN_LIBRARY_DIR: the HOST path Jellyfin sees as /media. Empty = the whole
# ${MEDIA_ROOT}. Set it to the video subtree when the media root also holds
# music/photos that Jellyfin has no business indexing.
JELLYFIN_IMAGE_TAG=10.10.7
JELLYFIN_PORT=8096
JELLYFIN_LIBRARY_DIR=

# Music: Navidrome (Subsonic API).
NAVIDROME_IMAGE_TAG=0.53.3
NAVIDROME_PORT=4533

# Podcasts + audiobooks: Audiobookshelf.
AUDIOBOOKSHELF_IMAGE_TAG=2.17.5
AUDIOBOOKSHELF_PORT=13378

# Passwords: Vaultwarden — HTTPS-only via the Caddyfile vault.* site (no LAN
# port on purpose). Flip signups on for first-account creation, then back off.
VAULTWARDEN_IMAGE_TAG=1.33.2
VAULTWARDEN_SIGNUPS_ALLOWED=false

# Home automation: Home Assistant (monthly YYYY.M releases) + Mosquitto (MQTT).
HOMEASSISTANT_IMAGE_TAG=2026.7
MOSQUITTO_IMAGE_TAG=2.0
MOSQUITTO_PORT=1883

# Files: Syncthing (set a GUI password on first visit to the web UI).
SYNCTHING_IMAGE_TAG=1.29.2
SYNCTHING_GUI_PORT=8384

# Quality-of-life: RSS, recipes, LAN dashboard.
FRESHRSS_IMAGE_TAG=1.25.0
FRESHRSS_PORT=8082
MEALIE_IMAGE_TAG=v2.4.2
MEALIE_PORT=9925
HOMEPAGE_IMAGE_TAG=v0.10.9
HOMEPAGE_PORT=3002

# Ops: diun — update NOTIFIER for the pinned images (never auto-updates).
# Endpoint targets the ntfy container over the bridge — enable the ntfy profile
# alongside, or point this at any external ntfy server.
DIUN_IMAGE_TAG=4.29.0
DIUN_NTFY_ENDPOINT=http://ntfy:80
DIUN_NTFY_TOPIC=homehub-updates

# ── Host-level credentials: NOT read by any container ───────────────────────
# These three ride .env because it is the one file the deploy pipeline already
# delivers to the box 0600 root:root, and because host scripts already know how
# to read one key out of it without sourcing the whole thing. Nothing in
# docker-compose.yml references them.
#
# OPERATOR_PASSWORD is the hub account's UNIX password (physical console + LAN
# xrdp if anyone ever opts the desktop back in). Store key OperatorPassword;
# AUTOINSTALL_PASSWORD_HASH in user-data is derived from the same value.
#
# ADDED HERE 2026-08-27, AND IT WAS A REAL GAP: the knob existed in
# FieldSchema.psd1 and the box-side readers existed, but no template carried the
# key — and Materialize-Deploy only fills keys a template already names. So the
# emitted .env never had it, and the value on the bench box got there by hand.
# A knob with no template line is emitted by nothing and reported by nothing.
OPERATOR_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD

# ── The two OPTIONAL FEATURES, both OFF in this repo ────────────────────────
# RULED BY THE OWNER 2026-08-27: "Deployer I'm okay with as long as it defaults
# IceDrive and remote desktop to off from its side, and gets configured to
# active from the HomeHub."
#
# These are ACTIVATION knobs. They decide what firstboot turns on; they do NOT
# decide what the image carries. Carriage is a BUILD decision (packages.optional
# .list via BAKE_OPTIONAL, and the icedrive.pin artifacts), and both halves are
# derived from ONE `Extras` declaration in HomeHub's config.homehub.psd1 so that
# they cannot disagree.
#
# THEY DISAGREED FOR A MONTH, WHICH IS WHY THE GATE EXISTS. The project told
# operators to "RDP in and sign in to IceDrive" while eleven of the AppImage's
# runtime libraries were in no image at all. Activation without carriage is an
# instruction nobody can follow; Materialize-Deploy now refuses to emit one.

# Whether firstboot installs and enables the RDP + minimal-XFCE session (SR-015)
# and, with it, the boot-time session that lets a GUI app run with nobody
# connected. `false` leaves the box headless: no X, no tcp/3389.
REMOTE_UI_ENABLED=false

# Which IceDrive client firstboot provisions, if any:
#   off       nothing. No client, no mount, no credential consumed.
#   appimage  the GUI client, autostarted inside the graphical session.
#             REQUIRES REMOTE_UI_ENABLED=true - it cannot run without a display.
#   cli       the headless CLI: no session needed, signs in once
#             non-interactively and FUSE-mounts at ICEDRIVE_MOUNTPOINT.
#             Proven working; see stack/icedrive/README.md for what it does and
#             does NOT do (it is a mount client, not a sync client).
# NEVER run both: two clients on one account fight over the same folders.
ICEDRIVE_MODE=off

# Where the CLI mounts the cloud. Ignored unless ICEDRIVE_MODE=cli.
ICEDRIVE_MOUNTPOINT=/srv/icedrive

# The IceDrive account, consumed ONCE by stack/icedrive/setup-icedrive.sh, which
# hands it to `IcedriveCLI -login/-password` at provisioning. Ruled a deploy
# secret by the Owner 2026-08-27, reversing the 2026-08-09 ruling on that
# ruling's own terms (it turned on "there is no CLI or API to hand it to").
#
# ONLY THE `cli` MODE USES THIS. The AppImage takes its credential typed into
# its own GUI over RDP and cannot be handed one from here.
#
# OPTIONAL AND BLANK BY DEFAULT. Left empty, setup-icedrive.sh installs the
# binary and stops: no sign-in, no mount, no unit enabled.
ICEDRIVE_USER=
ICEDRIVE_PASSWORD=

# ── IceDrive profile capture (reimage recovery, 2026-08-29) ──────────────────
# The key that encrypts the hub's captured IceDrive sign-in - the API token, the
# saved credential and the web-session cookies - before it rides the nightly
# backup onto the removable archive drive as the `icedrive` set.
#
# THE TWO HALVES TRAVEL ON DIFFERENT MEDIA, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. The
# ciphertext is on the backup drive; this key is here, in a file that is 0600
# root and is materialised onto the INSTALL STICK. Either alone recovers
# nothing. A plaintext profile on a drive built to be carried away would hand a
# thief a live cloud session rather than only the documents the drive already
# holds - which is why an adversarial review made this separation a condition of
# automating the capture at all.
#
# EMPTY = the capture REFUSES TO RUN. It does not fall back to writing the
# profile in the clear, and it says so. A box with IceDrive off never reaches it.
ICEDRIVE_PROFILE_KEY=

# Panel private access: opt in only after gateway + broker provisioning and
# validate-panel-access.py succeed. See panel-access/README.md. Default legacy.
PANEL_ACCESS_ENABLED=false
PANEL_ACCESS_IMAGE=node:22-bookworm-slim
PANEL_ACCESS_MODE=read-protected
PANEL_IDLE_MS=120000
PANEL_HARD_MS=900000

# ── AI CLI service (SR-019, SN-016; A40 ratified 2026-09-09) ─────────────────
# A plain hub service — no container — that takes messages from the other
# containers and on-box jobs and answers them by running ONE headless CLI
# session on the household subscription. A container was ruled OUT because it
# delivers no containment here: the credential has to come from a home
# directory a human logged into interactively, so a container would have to
# mount that home and the isolation is punctured at the only point that
# mattered. Containment instead comes from the four knobs below plus the
# read-only registry, and every one of them is asserted by a test
# (tests/test_ai_cli_service.py, stack/ai-cli/tests/ai-cli-guards.test.sh).
#
# NO CREDENTIAL IS DECLARED HERE AND NONE EVER WILL BE. The subscription token
# is minted by `claude setup-token` inside an RDP session as AI_CLI_USER and
# lives at ~AI_CLI_USER/.claude/.credentials.json, mode 0600 — it does not
# survive a reimage, the same posture as IceDrive, and the reimage checklist
# names the re-login step.

# Off by default. `false` means the account is not created, the unit is not
# installed and nothing listens. Gated exactly like REMOTE_UI_ENABLED.
AI_CLI_ENABLED=false

# THE DEDICATED UNPRIVILEGED ACCOUNT, and it must never be `hub`. `hub` is the
# box's only account with a real shell and it carries (ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL, so a
# service running as `hub` and driving an agent chosen by a caller is arbitrary
# code execution as a passwordless-sudo user. setup-ai-cli.sh REFUSES `hub` and
# `root`, and refuses any account that has since been put in sudo/docker/adm or
# named in a sudoers rule.
AI_CLI_USER=homehub-ai

# LOOPBACK OR THE DOCKER BRIDGE, NEVER THE LAN. Validated before the socket is
# created: 127.0.0.0/8, ::1, or an address a LOCAL DOCKER BRIDGE INTERFACE
# (docker0 / br-*) is actually carrying. NOT a range: the first version of this
# guard accepted the whole of 172.16.0.0/12, and a household LAN on
# 172.20.0.0/16 sits inside that range, so "the docker bridge" admitted the LAN
# (cross-review 2026-09-09, V3). On a box with no docker bridge, only loopback
# binds. `0.0.0.0` is refused by name — it is the edit that puts the
# household's interactive credential on the LAN without looking like a LAN
# address. A hostname is refused too.
AI_CLI_BIND=127.0.0.1
AI_CLI_PORT=8791

# A SCRATCH WORKING DIRECTORY PER REQUEST lives under here: one mkdtemp child
# per request, 0700, removed on every exit path including a timeout. These CLIs
# are coding agents whose sandbox is scoped to the working directory and which
# load whatever CLAUDE.md and skills the starting tree holds, so a caller-chosen
# job must never begin in a real tree.
AI_CLI_SCRATCH_ROOT=/var/lib/homehub-ai/scratch

# Which routes a caller may name, and where they are declared. The registry is
# a CATALOG; the enable-list is the CONSENT, and an absent one is "no routes"
# rather than "every route".
AI_CLI_REGISTRY=/opt/homehub/stack/ai-cli/agents.registry.csv
AI_CLI_ENABLED_ROUTES=/opt/homehub/stack/ai-cli/routes-enabled

# Per-session timeout and the per-route cooldown applied after a timeout or a
# session with no typed result. The cooldown is per registry row and
# independent by construction, so one exhausted route never mutes another.
#
# SET THE TIMEOUT AGAINST THE MEASURED FLOOR, NOT A GUESS. `--bare` — the fast
# startup meant for scripts — ignores the OAuth token and requires an API key,
# so a service on the subscription cannot use it and pays full startup per
# call: hooks, MCP servers, skills and CLAUDE.md all load every invocation.
# Measured 2026-09-09 (dev PC, claude 2.1.201, trivial prompt, three runs):
# 2.9-4.1s wall of which 1.6-2.2s is non-model startup. See ai-cli/README.md.
AI_CLI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300
AI_CLI_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=120

# The pacing after a SUCCESSFUL call. The cooldown above only ever paced
# failures, so a hot loop could launch session after session on the household
# subscription; this is the small gap between successful launches of the same
# row. 0 disables it.
AI_CLI_SUCCESS_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=5

# BOUNDS FOR A SMALL ALWAYS-ON BOX (cross-review 2026-09-09). The reviewed
# version had none of these: every POST spawned a thread and a CLI process
# allowed the full timeout, with no cap on how many ran at once and no ceiling
# on the body it would read first.
#   MAX_CONCURRENT   sessions allowed to run at the same time across ALL routes
#                    (the box, not the row, is the scarce thing). Extra requests
#                    get 503 immediately rather than queueing.
#   MAX_BODY_BYTES   Content-Length ceiling, refused BEFORE a byte is read.
#   SOCKET_TIMEOUT   seconds a connection may idle, so a slow client cannot pin
#                    a handler thread.
#   MAX_CONNECTIONS  concurrent connections the server will accept at all.
AI_CLI_MAX_CONCURRENT=1
AI_CLI_MAX_BODY_BYTES=262144
AI_CLI_SOCKET_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
AI_CLI_MAX_CONNECTIONS=8

# WHERE THE PINNED CLI BINARIES ARE FOUND, and the only place they are looked
# for. The command template names `claude` or `codex` and nothing else; the
# service resolves that name HERE rather than on the inherited PATH, and
# refuses a resolved binary that is writable by group or other. A registry row
# cannot set PATH (or HOME, or CODEX_HOME) to move the target: the Env cell is
# an allow-list. Change this only if the Owner installed the CLIs elsewhere.
AI_CLI_BIN_PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# ── AI-usage feeder (SR-021, SN-016; IF-013) ────────────────────────────────
# Reads how much of each AI subscription has been consumed and posts it to
# NagLight as gauges. It is a plain hub service on a timer — no container, for
# the same reason the AI CLI service is not one: two of the three sources sit
# beside a credential a human minted interactively into a home directory.
#
# NO CREDENTIAL IS DECLARED HERE AND NONE EVER WILL BE, exactly as for the AI
# CLI service. The feeder READS the sign-ins the Owner already made as
# AI_USAGE_USER_ACCOUNT and never writes one — `open_for_write` refuses every
# path but the state file below, the unit mounts that home read-only, and
# tests/test_ai_usage_feeder.py runs a whole cycle inside a throwaway HOME and
# fails if a single byte under it changed.
#
# THREE SOURCES, EACH VERIFIED WITH ONE REAL CALL BEFORE ITS PARSER EXISTED
# (2026-09-09): codex via `codex app-server` JSON-RPC `account/rateLimits/read`;
# Claude via the OAuth usage endpoint with its beta header; OpenCode Go via
# GET https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/usage. Gemini is OUT — deferred as E11.

# Off by default. There is no compose profile to join (this is not a
# container), so the knob IS the gate — the same shape as AI_CLI_ENABLED and
# REMOTE_UI_ENABLED. `false` means no unit, no timer, and nothing read.
AI_USAGE_ENABLED=false

# THE ONE IDENTITY THESE GAUGES ARE POSTED FOR, as the stable Google `sub` —
# the same value as NAGLIGHT_USER / TRACKER_MIRROR_USER / TRACKER_DRIVE_USER.
# BLANK IS A REFUSAL, NOT A DEFAULT: with the feeder enabled and this empty,
# setup-ai-usage.sh refuses to install and the feeder refuses to run, both
# saying why. There is no "the only user" fallback, because a single-user box
# today is a two-user box after one oauth2-proxy login, and a usage gauge on
# the wrong person's board is a lie about their own subscription.
AI_USAGE_USER=

# The UNIX account the feeder runs as, and the account whose home the vendor
# sign-ins live in. Normally the same as AI_CLI_USER, and it must be: the
# credentials are where the interactive login put them. `hub` and `root` are
# refused. The account is created and certified by setup-ai-cli.sh — this
# feeder never creates one.
AI_USAGE_USER_ACCOUNT=homehub-ai

# WHERE THE GAUGES GO. Required; there is no default, because the tracker is
# bridge-only (no host publish) so the right value depends on this box: its
# loopback from inside, or its docker-bridge address from the host. The feeder
# REFUSES any other host — a LAN address, a public name, or the https route
# through oauth2-proxy, which would overwrite X-Forwarded-User and post the
# gauges as somebody else. Same lesson as AI_CLI_BIND: an address a bridge
# actually carries, not a range that merely contains one.
AI_USAGE_FEED_URL=
# Bearer token for /api/feed (TRACKER_API_TOKEN on the tracker). Blank sends no
# Authorization header, which is right for a tracker that requires none.
AI_USAGE_FEED_TOKEN=

# Which sources to read, comma-separated: codex, claude, opencode. An unknown
# name is REFUSED rather than skipped. Drop a source the Owner has not signed
# in — a permanently unavailable gauge teaches people to ignore unavailable
# gauges, which is the one thing this feeder must not do.
AI_USAGE_SOURCES=codex,claude,opencode

# Where each source's credential is READ FROM (never written). Codex has no
# entry on purpose: `codex app-server` reads its own ~/.codex/auth.json in a
# child process and no token passes through the feeder at all.
AI_USAGE_CLAUDE_CREDENTIALS=/home/homehub-ai/.claude/.credentials.json
AI_USAGE_OPENCODE_AUTH=/home/homehub-ai/.local/share/opencode/auth.json

# The `claude-code/<version>` user agent the OAuth usage endpoint is called
# with. Bump it when the CLI is upgraded on the box.
AI_USAGE_CLAUDE_VERSION=2.0.0

# The ONE file this feeder may write: the last reading per gauge, so a source
# that fails can repost the number that WAS true at the stamp it was true
# instead of inventing one. Percentages and timestamps only — asserted.
AI_USAGE_STATE_FILE=/var/lib/homehub-ai/usage-state.json

# Per-source timeout, whole seconds. A wedged source must become an
# "unavailable" gauge, not a stuck unit.
AI_USAGE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# ── Weight feeder (SR-022, SN-040; IF-014) ──────────────────────────────────
# Posts one body-weight reading as the `weight` gauge, against the goal the
# person declared in THEIR OWN definitions. It REPLACES NagLight's manual
# feeders/weight-manual.ps1 by posting the same gauge id, so the two never
# stand two bars side by side. A plain hub service on a timer, no container —
# the vendor half is a refresh token a human minted through a browser.
#
# THE VENDOR HALF IS BLOCKED ON AN OWNER ACTION AND THE FEEDER SAYS SO. Google
# Health API v4 was verified against Google on 2026-09-09 (the API, the Weight
# type, the route and the scope — see stack/weight/README.md for the calls and
# the responses), but no authenticated call has ever been made, so the feeder
# contains NO PARSER and posts "unavailable" until one has. That is correct
# behaviour, not a fault to chase.
#
# NOTE THE SCOPE IS NOT WEIGHT-SPECIFIC. The only scope that admits the Weight
# data type is googlehealth.health_metrics_and_measurements.readonly, which
# also grants blood glucose, body fat, oxygen saturation and heart-rate
# metrics. Consenting to it hands this box all of those.

# Off by default. There is no compose profile to join (this is not a
# container), so the knob IS the gate — the same shape as AI_USAGE_ENABLED.
WEIGHT_ENABLED=false

# THE ONE IDENTITY THIS GAUGE IS POSTED FOR, as the stable Google `sub` — the
# same value as NAGLIGHT_USER / TRACKER_DRIVE_USER / AI_USAGE_USER. BLANK IS A
# REFUSAL, NOT A DEFAULT, and it matters more here than for a usage gauge: a
# body weight on the wrong household member's board is a private measurement
# displayed to the wrong person on a wall in a shared room.
WEIGHT_USER=

# The UNIX account the feeder runs as, and whose home the refresh token lives
# in. Unlike the usage feeder this account is NOT shared with the AI CLIs:
# this credential is minted for this purpose, so it gets a home with nothing
# else in it. Created by setup-weight.sh. `hub` and `root` are refused.
WEIGHT_USER_ACCOUNT=homehub-weight

# WHERE THE GAUGE GOES. Required; there is no default, because the tracker is
# bridge-only (no host publish) so the right value depends on this box: its
# loopback from inside, or its docker-bridge address from the host. The feeder
# REFUSES any other host — a LAN address, a public name, or the https route
# through oauth2-proxy, which would overwrite X-Forwarded-User and post the
# reading as somebody else.
WEIGHT_FEED_URL=
# Bearer token for /api/feed (TRACKER_API_TOKEN on the tracker). Blank sends no
# Authorization header, which is right for a tracker that requires none.
WEIGHT_FEED_TOKEN=

# WHERE THE GOAL LIVES, AND IT IS NOT A KNOB ON THIS BOX. SN-040: "the goal
# lives in the user's definitions so it syncs like everything else". So this
# names the DIRECTORY, not the number — in multi-user mode it is
#   <tracker data root>/<the Google sub>/definitions
# and the goal is a top-level `weight_goal_lb: 180` in the frontmatter of one
# definitions file. NagLight ignores unknown top-level keys, so nothing there
# has to change for the goal to live beside the things the person tracks.
#
# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO WEIGHT_GOAL KNOB TO FALL BACK ON. A goal on the hub
# would need an SSH session and a redeploy to change, would not travel with the
# rest of the person's tracker, and would be a second home for the household's
# intent — which is how this repo's /opt/homehub drift started. With no goal
# declared the feeder posts NOTHING and says which file to put it in.
#
# KNOWN GAP, AND IT IS A NagLight DEPENDENCY (see stack/weight/README.md). In
# Drive FOLDER mode the .md bytes are staged verbatim and the key rides along.
# In SHEET mode — which this household runs — internal/defsheet REGENERATES the
# .md from a fixed item-column list and drops unknown columns, so the goal
# would be erased by the first sync after someone edits the sheet. Making it
# survive needs a change in NagLight, not here.
WEIGHT_DEFINITIONS_DIR=

# Which source to read. Unknown names are REFUSED rather than skipped.
WEIGHT_SOURCE=google-health

# The Google Health OAuth refresh token, READ and never written. It is minted
# interactively by the Owner at a browser and does NOT survive a reimage.
# Its own directory, outside the state file's, for the same reason
# TRACKER_DRIVE_TOKEN_DIR is its own volume rather than a path under /data:
# a token in a directory something else sweeps up gets swept up.
WEIGHT_TOKEN_FILE=/var/lib/homehub-weight/tokens/google-health-token.json

# The ONE file this feeder may write: the last reading, so a source that fails
# can repost the number that WAS true at the stamp it was true instead of
# inventing one. A weight and a timestamp only — asserted by test.
WEIGHT_STATE_FILE=/var/lib/homehub-weight/weight-state.json

# Source timeout, whole seconds. A wedged source must become an "unavailable"
# gauge, not a stuck unit.
WEIGHT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
